For bachelor's students (BS and BBA), both A+ and A earn 4.00 points and are rated excellent. A- earns 3.67 and B+ earns 3.33, both rated very good. B is worth 3.00 (good), B- 2.67 (average), C+ 2.33 (below average) and C 2.00 (adequate). C- at 1.67, D+ at 1.33 and D at 1.00 are all counted as passes. Master's students (MS and MBA) see the same points from A+ down to C, and A+ and A are still excellent, but the other descriptions are stricter: A- is very good, B+ is good, B is average, B- is below average, C+ is adequate and C is only a pass. Doctoral students have a shorter scale that stops at B- (2.67, below average). A+ and A give 4.00 (excellent), A- gives 3.67 (very good), B+ gives 3.33 (good) and B gives 3.00 (average). Some codes apply in every programme. F means you failed, and FA means you failed because your attendance was too low; both carry zero points. CR is used while research is still continuing, I means the course is incomplete, and W means you withdrew. CR, I and W are left out when either average is worked out. A W stays visible on your transcript like other grades. S and U are used for doctoral thesis work alone and never affect the CGPA.

